<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- A File Provider extension is always sandboxed; the system will
         not load one that is not. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The extension reaches the sync agent over its local control
         socket. That is a network client connection even though it
         never leaves the machine, and without this the connect fails
         with a refusal the sandbox does not otherwise explain. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Something down the Rust side's dependency tree allocates
         executable memory, and the hardened runtime refuses `MAP_JIT`
         without this. The failure is a Rust panic — "mmap(MAP_JIT)
         failed" — inside the very first call the extension makes, which
         before the FFI guard was an abort and reached macOS as "the
         connection to service was invalidated": no mention of JIT,
         entitlements, or the call.

         macOS 26 allowed it and macOS 27 does not, so the same signed
         binary worked on one Mac and died on the next. The containing
         app already carries this for WKWebView's JavaScript JIT; the
         extension is a separate program and needs its own. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The live tree is on disk outside the container: the agent
         materializes into it, and the extension reads and writes it.
         The app group is the shared boundary both halves agree on. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>group.app.fasttrackstudio.task</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Roots can live anywhere a person keeps their projects —
         including an external drive, which is the whole reason the
         agent takes a "roots" flag. A user-selected read-write scope is
         what lets the extension follow them there. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The extension reads and writes the live tree directly, and the
         sandbox otherwise forbids it: a root at ~/Task/<project> came
         back as NSCocoaErrorDomain 257, "you don't have permission to
         view it", and the folder listed empty with no other sign of
         why. `user-selected.read-write` does not help — it grants what
         a person picked in an open panel, and nobody picks anything
         here.

         Scoped to `/` because a root is wherever its owner keeps it.
         The agent takes a `roots` directory precisely so a machine with
         a small disk can put projects on an external drive, so any
         narrower path would be a limit disguised as a permission.

         **This is a debt, and it is the reason a Mac App Store build
         cannot ship.** Apple grants temporary exceptions for
         Developer-ID distribution, which is what the DMG is, and
         reviews them out of App Store submissions. The way out is to
         stop touching files here at all: the agent already owns the
         store, and an extension that asked it to enumerate and to
         stage content would need no file access whatsoever. That is
         the design this should converge on — see docs/sync.md. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write</key>
    <array><string>/</string></array>
</dict>
</plist>
